        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Matthew</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Enter into thy closet, and when thou hast shut thy door, pray to thy Father which is in secret&#x27; — Cyril sees the tameion (inner room) as both literal solitude AND the soul&#x27;s own inwardness. The kekleismenē-door shuts out the gallery, opens the inner colloquy. Tewahedo monastic cell-prayer (sǝlot bä-bēt) and the Hesychast-Tewahedo-bridge inherit this.</p></aside>',
