        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Matthew</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>The Lord&#x27;s Prayer is the prayer of the adopted: &#x27;our Father&#x27; presupposes the new birth into the Son&#x27;s filial relation. Cyril reads the petitions as the Pentecost-anticipating Christian life in miniature — kingdom-come, will-done, super-substantial-bread (epiousios, with Eucharistic resonance), forgiveness-as-forgiven, deliverance-from-the-evil-one. The Tewahedo Qǝddāse opens its anaphoral prayers with the Abba dialogue.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Matthew (fragments), preserved in J.A. Cramer, Catenae Graecorum Patrum in Novum Testamentum, Vol. I: In Evangelia S. Matthaei et S. Marci (Oxford: University Press, 1840). PD. Greek text also in PG 72 cols. 365-474.",
