        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Matthew</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you&#x27; — Cyril sees the present-imperative triplet (aiteite-zēteite-krouete) as commanding perseverant rather than one-time prayer. Each verb intensifies the previous: asking is verbal, seeking is active, knocking is bodily-present.</p></aside>',
