        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Matthew</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Tell ye the daughter of Sion, Behold, thy King cometh unto thee, meek, and sitting upon an ass, and a colt the foal of an ass&#x27; — Cyril marks Zech 9:9 as the prophetic-fulfillment cite: the king-comes-meek (praos), not as Roman-imperator on warhorse but as Jewish-davidic-redeemer on humble mount. The duality (donkey + colt) is harmonized via the standard rabbinic-prophetic-parallelism. Tewahedo Hosanna-feast hymnody (Hosanna Sunday before Pasch) explicitly cites Zech 9:9 + Mt 21:5 + Jn 12:15 as the triple-prophetic-fulfillment foundation.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Matthew (fragments), preserved in J.A. Cramer, Catenae Graecorum Patrum in Novum Testamentum, Vol. I: In Evangelia S. Matthaei et S. Marci (Oxford: University Press, 1840). PD. Greek text also in PG 72 cols. 365-474.",
