        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Matthew</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And the high priest answered and said unto him, I adjure thee by the living God, that thou tell us whether thou be the Christ, the Son of God&#x27; — Cyril marks Caiaphas&#x27;s exorkizō se (I-adjure-thee) as the legal-oath formula that compels Christ&#x27;s reply. The convergence is providential: the highest-priest of the Old Covenant unwittingly elicits the explicit-Christological confession from the True-High-Priest. Christ&#x27;s su eipas (thou hast said, Mt 26:64) is affirmative-with-irony — Caiaphas himself has uttered the truth. Tewahedo Holy-Friday liturgy reads the Sanhedrin-trial sequence as the climactic-Christological-confession-under-duress.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Matthew (fragments), preserved in J.A. Cramer, Catenae Graecorum Patrum in Novum Testamentum, Vol. I: In Evangelia S. Matthaei et S. Marci (Oxford: University Press, 1840). PD. Greek text also in PG 72 cols. 365-474.",
