        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Matthew</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani&#x27; — Cyril&#x27;s impassible-passion locus. The cry is not the Son&#x27;s despair (he, being God, cannot despair) but the assumed human nature voicing Ps 22:1 from within forsakenness, that the Psalm — opening in dereliction, closing in vindication — might be answered in the Resurrection. Tewahedo Hāmus Sǝgǝd (Holy Thursday) chants pair Mt 27:46 with the full Ps 22 reading.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Matthew (fragments), preserved in J.A. Cramer, Catenae Graecorum Patrum in Novum Testamentum, Vol. I: In Evangelia S. Matthaei et S. Marci (Oxford: University Press, 1840). PD. Greek text also in PG 72 cols. 365-474.",
